Myth: Long ago, threads held no color—everything woven was gray as stone. The world itself seemed drained of joy, and people's hearts grew heavy with monotony.

There lived a young weaver named Kiran who refused to accept this colorless fate. Each day, he experimented with different materials—crushing flowers, berries, and minerals—but nothing would stain the stubborn gray threads.

One dawn, Kiran climbed the highest mountain, hoping to find answers in the clouds. There he discovered the Dawn Spirit painting the sunrise with her fingers, pulling colors from her own essence.

"Please," Kiran begged, "teach me to bring color to my world."

The Dawn Spirit smiled sadly. "Color cannot be taken—it must be given freely through sacrifice. What will you offer?"

Kiran thought of his village's gray faces. "My voice," he whispered. "I will give my voice so others may have beauty."

The Dawn Spirit was moved. She wept, and her tears became the first dyes—crimson, gold, sapphire, and emerald. Kiran could no longer speak, but his hands wove colors more vibrant than words ever could.

Today, when weavers create something beautiful, they work in reverent silence—honoring Kiran's sacrifice that painted the world.
